 In C++, you can utilize the `for_each` function from the Standard Template Library (STL) to run the same code inline on multiple named variables. Here's a simple example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <functional>

int main() {
    int a = 5, b = 10, c = 15;

    std::vector<int> numbers = {a, b, c};
    std::for_each(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), [](int num) {
        std::cout << num * num << std::endl;
    });

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the lambda function `[](int num) { std::cout << num * num << std::endl; }` is applied to each element in the `numbers` vector, which contains the variables `a`, `b`, and `c`. This way, you can run the same code inline on multiple variables without repeating the code for each variable.

